    {
      "content": "A line-citation gate that only checks 'the cited line exists and is non-blank' cannot see a citation pointing at the WRONG declaration; a shifted (or doubly shifted) citation almost always still lands on some non-blank line. Checking a citation that NAMES a declaration against that declaration's span (leading doc comment through the line before the next declaration) found 327 silently stale citations in a tree whose existing gate had been green throughout. Key design rules that kept it trustworthy: only a name immediately adjacent to the citation can FAIL it (names elsewhere in the sentence can only PASS); names not declared in the target file are 'unverifiable', never guessed; a span, not the keyword line, is the target.",
